.TH CON 4
.SH NAME
con \- the console server: the text terminal, keyboard and mouse (port "console")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR console .
A boot module, started first by
.BR init (8)
with device rights; restarted if it ends.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B con
is the text terminal of SIEOS and the owner of the keyboard and the mouse.
On x86-64 it drives the VGA text screen (80 by 25), the PS/2 keyboard and
mouse (QEMU's absolute pointer when there is one) and the first serial port,
COM1.
On arm64 it drives the serial port the device tree names as the console (a
PL011) and virtio keyboards and tablets.
USB and Bluetooth keyboards and mice reach it through
.BR CON_INJECT .
.PP
What a program prints goes to the screen and, through the kernel's log
.RB ( sys_debug (2)),
to the serial line, whole, so its lines never mix with the kernel's.
Typed lines are edited by the line discipline shared with the desktop's
terminals and
.BR sshd (8)
(user/lib/tty.c): Backspace edits, Enter completes the line, Ctrl-C drops it
and sends SIGINT to the foreground job, Ctrl-D sends what is there (nothing:
end of input).
On the VGA screen it understands the escape sequences ESC [ H (home),
ESC [ J (clear the screen), ESC [ K (clear to the end of the line) and
ESC [ 1 m, ESC [ 4 m, ESC [ 0 m (bright, cyan, plain again: what
.BR man (1)
shows in bold and underlined); others are ignored.
.PP
A program's console is the one in its identity
.RB ( sys_ident (2)):
"port" or "port#channel", the channel sent in w[3]; empty means "console".
A terminal window of the desktop or an SSH session is such a console.
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B CON_WRITE
sbuf: the text to print. \-> bytes written.
.TP
.B CON_READ
w[1] = the most bytes wanted; a typed line comes in rbuf. \-> bytes (0: end
of input). One reader at a time (\-EBUSY otherwise).
.TP
.B CON_ECHO
w[1] = 1: show typed characters; 0: hide them (passwords);
.BR CON_RAW :
each key as it comes, unedited and unshown (Enter is CR, Backspace DEL, the
arrows ESC [ A..D).
.TP
.B CON_FG
w[1] = the process group of the job the shell runs now (0: none), the target
of Ctrl-C. Only the group's user, or root. \-> 0.
.TP
.B CON_INPUT
(root: the desktop) become the owner of the keyboard and mouse: their events
go to the caller, as con_event_t[] in rbuf \-> how many. The serial port
stays a text console. If the owner ends, the keyboard comes back.
.TP
.B CON_HASINPUT
\-> 1 if there is a keyboard or pointer, 0 if not.
.TP
.B CON_INJECT
(root: the USB server, btd, i2chid, ipts) sbuf = con_inject_t[]: keys as
set-1 scancodes, relative mouse moves, absolute tablet positions, buttons and
wheel. None: "a keyboard or pointer is here". \-> 0.
.TP
.B CON_MOUSE
(root: the desktop) w[1] = the pointer's speed in % (25..400), w[2], w[3] =
the screen's width and height. \-> 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR atlas (4),
.BR usb (4),
.BR sshd (8),
.BR intro (4)
.SH BUGS
Programs cannot ask the terminal's size.
